$\begin{align}
\text{vertex's }t\text{-coordinate}&=\dfrac{(\goldD{-15})+(\goldD{-4})}{2}
\\\\
&=\blueD{-\dfrac{19}{2}}
\end{align}$

Now we can find the vertex's $y$-coordinate by evaluating $f\left(\blueD{-\dfrac{19}{2}}\right)$:

$\begin{align}
f\left(\blueD{-\dfrac{19}{2}}\right)&=\left(\blueD{-\dfrac{19}{2}}\right)^2+19\left(\blueD{-\dfrac{19}{2}}\right)+60
\\\\
&=\dfrac{361}{4}-\dfrac{361}{2}+60
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{121}{4}
\end{align}$
